{{Culture of Okaiken}}

[[File:Toyozawa_Castle.png|thumb|right|300px|[[Toyozawa Castle]], built in 1619 on [[Yagikoshu]], exemplifies the monumental ''tenshu'' keep and ''shoin-zukuri'' palace architecture of the early [[Honda clan|Honda Shogunate]].]]

'''Architecture in Okaiken''' ({{nihongo|岡井建築|Okaiken kenchiku}}), historically known as '''Sanese architecture''' ({{nihongo|三人建築|Sanjin kenchiku}}), encompasses the building traditions, sacred spatial practices, vernacular dwellings, and urban megastructures that have developed across the [[Sanese Archipelago]] over more than ten millennia. Shaped by the Y-shaped archipelago's mountainous topography, dense forest cover, frequent seismic shocks along the [[Shendan Trench]], and seasonal typhoons sweeping across the [[Inland Sea of Mitoyagi]], traditional Sanese architecture is typified by timber post-and-lintel structures elevated slightly above the earth, crowned by sweeping tiled, thatched, or cypress-bark roofs and divided internally by non-load-bearing sliding partitions ({{nihongo|''fusuma''|襖}} and {{nihongo|''shōji''|障子}}).

Unlike continental building traditions that historically privileged axial stone permanence and rigid separation between structure and landscape, Sanese architecture is grounded in the cosmological framework of [[Mythology of Okaiken|Okaiken's creation mythology]], the animistic threshold theology of [[Shoto]], the monastic geometry of [[Baiduism]], and the aesthetic canons of [[Art in Okaiken]]. Within this tradition, every major edifice is conceived as a living enactment of the [[Mythology of Okaiken#The Stone Pounding Cycle|Stone Pounding Cycle]]—balancing a compacted dry-stone or volcanic plinth below ({{nihongo|''tsuchi-uchi''|土打ち|"the pounding"}}), a flexible, mortise-and-tenon timber frame that yields to seismic vibration in the middle ({{nihongo|''chi-yurumi''|地緩み|"the loosening"}}), and a soaring hipped-and-gabled roof that vents heat and pressure toward the heavens above ({{nihongo|''tsuchi-hanashi''|土放し|"the release"}}). Furthermore, Sanese builders historically rejected any division between architecture and garden design, treating interior rooms, covered verandas ({{nihongo|''engawa''|縁側}}), and exterior courtyards as a single {{nihongo|"garden-building continuum"|庭建連続|niwa-tate renzoku}}.

Over the past century and a half, Okaiken has synthesized this inherited timber and stone vocabulary with modern structural engineering. Following the [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871, the post-war [[Sanese Metabolism|Metabolist movement]] of the 1960s, and the ferro-concrete civic megastructures of the socialist Republic Era (1968–2011), contemporary Sanese architecture following the 2011 [[Second Imperial Restoration (Okaiken)|Jutsu Restoration]] has spearheaded a nationwide {{nihongo|"Wood Renaissance"|木のルネサンス|Ki no Runesansu}}, combining ancient nail-free joinery with cross-laminated timber (CLT) engineering, algorithmic seismic damping, and coastal ecological restoration.

==Cosmological and aesthetic foundations==
{{See also|Mythology of Okaiken|Art in Okaiken}}

===The Stone Pounding Cycle and tectonic structure===
